n to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-10x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5354f2df-c517-580b-bf58-a3327b4043a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5354f2df-c517-580b-bf58-a3327b4043a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305957} :answer-field/id #uuid "6676f0cc-6fe9-5a46-aba8-ca872959a1d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307846}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b67c45f-7435-5b3b-b8a6-35875cc6d705" :question/math-academy-id "q-333160" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}) & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77d645f3-858a-5441-98ca-c391f11f6446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d645f3-858a-5441-98ca-c391f11f6446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306289} :answer-field/id #uuid "8099e683-1503-5e10-8c12-f1fec8b2ad2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307925}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a04ef1bf-4028-5cae-b670-2200bafd2729" :question/math-academy-id "q-333171" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha {e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x})-3(\\alpha {e}^{x}) & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-3)\\alpha {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4 & =1 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c009c47c-0010-58f0-af3f-4c58afce7657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{x}+7x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c009c47c-0010-58f0-af3f-4c58afce7657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{x}+7x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363744} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bffb413-d127-5384-b66a-53461a8d5549" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363743}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c0d25a5-20ac-54d3-b942-54173b2e140e" :question/math-academy-id "q-332942" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=7{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=4\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=7{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $7{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+x){e}^{x})-(\\alpha x{e}^{x}) & =7{e}^{x} \\\\ (1+x-x)\\alpha {e}^{x} & =7{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{x} & =7{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=7x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+7x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =4 \\\\ A & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=4{e}^{x}+7x{e}^{x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf807d62-ca51-5961-a553-e83da1486686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf807d62-ca51-5961-a553-e83da1486686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363760} :answer-field/id #uuid "e122ceda-ade9-50d7-a34b-de3368ba119a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363759}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6cac7352-0dbf-5dae-970e-a3b99343c574" :question/math-academy-id "q-333174" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=4{e}^{5x},\\,y(0)=-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=4{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}) & =4{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =4{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =4{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05391ed2-1af2-51c2-88ba-90a0af353d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05391ed2-1af2-51c2-88ba-90a0af353d2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531842} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa06a60-3d8f-5b52-8f06-5733fb800f30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531850}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "011c879a-fc3f-5230-b160-60ca53316234" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe examine both exponential forcing terms. The first exponential is a term in the complementary solution, so its trial term is multiplied by $x$; the other exponential is not. We assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′}+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x})+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})-3(\\alpha x{e}^{3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x}+(2-3)\\beta {e}^{2x} & =-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x}-\\beta {e}^{2x} & =-{e}^{3x}+3{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-1,\\qquad -\\beta =3.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-1,\\qquad \\beta =-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0-3 & =-1 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{3x}-x{e}^{3x}-3{e}^{2x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531843 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9518a01e-5e06-5c74-bbc4-71d8c76b9c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{94}{11}{e}^{-5x}+12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9518a01e-5e06-5c74-bbc4-71d8c76b9c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{94}{11}{e}^{-5x}+12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531852} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7d5d707-9631-5fbb-954a-04917c307af2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "155bc372-bd60-53e4-b6c8-19b23fcc0aaa" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x},\\,y(0)=8\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe examine both exponential forcing terms. The first exponential is a term in the complementary solution, so its trial term is multiplied by $x$; the other exponential is not. We assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′}+6\\beta {e}^{6x} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+6\\beta {e}^{6x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{6x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{6x}) & =12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(6+5)\\beta {e}^{6x} & =12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+11\\beta {e}^{6x} & =12{e}^{-5x}-6{e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12,\\qquad 11\\beta =-6.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12,\\qquad \\beta =-\\frac{6}{11}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0-\\frac{6}{11} & =8 \\\\ A & =\\frac{94}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{94}{11}{e}^{-5x}+12x{e}^{-5x}-\\frac{6}{11}{e}^{6x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b8869f-a2ef-55c9-b294-a409bd69a909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{-3x}-8x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91b8869f-a2ef-55c9-b294-a409bd69a909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{-3x}-8x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531851} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e84de7c-53fc-5f01-bfdc-1ad4ad24d439" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531845}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62226b80-0daa-5981-9200-5cc4183b2f0b" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-8{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-x},\\,y(0)=-4\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-8{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe examine both exponential forcing terms. The first exponential is a term in the complementary solution, so its trial term is multiplied by $x$; the other exponential is not. We assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′}-\\beta {e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x})-\\beta {e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}-\\beta {e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}-\\beta {e}^{-x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{-x}) & =-8{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+(-1+3)\\beta {e}^{-x} & =-8{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{-x} & =-8{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-8,\\qquad 2\\beta =4.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-8,\\qquad \\beta =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-8x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-8x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0+2 & =-4 \\\\ A & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-6{e}^{-3x}-8x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{-x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e0410f9-5db5-5fda-8d88-144e5b3004c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e0410f9-5db5-5fda-8d88-144e5b3004c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531846} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bf99e47-670f-558a-9e23-5e7fa9452cc5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531844}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f7b2bb6-e670-5a7b-8c29-a0c6dc5313b5" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=4\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe examine both exponential forcing terms. Neither exponential is a term in the complementary solution. We assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\alpha {e}^{-5x}-2\\beta {e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5\\alpha {e}^{-5x}-2\\beta {e}^{-2x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{-2x}) & =6{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (-5+3)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(-2+3)\\beta {e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha =6,\\qquad \\beta =2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-3,\\qquad \\beta =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3+2 & =4 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-5x}+2{e}^{-2x}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68609974-e2b8-547e-8dc8-ccf67124fc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5x}-9x{e}^{5x}+\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68609974-e2b8-547e-8dc8-ccf67124fc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5x}-9x{e}^{5x}+\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531848} :answer-field/id #uuid "dce5e95d-a2a1-5029-9a09-f5a50acc31f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531855}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a78885a6-4367-59e3-8a71-6384757be04b" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=-9{e}^{5x}-10{e}^{-x},\\,y(0)=4\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=-9{e}^{5x}-10{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe examine both exponential forcing terms. The first exponential is a term in the complementary solution, so its trial term is multiplied by $x$; the other exponential is not. We assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x}+\\beta {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}+\\beta {e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′}-\\beta {e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x})-\\beta {e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}-\\beta {e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}-\\beta {e}^{-x})-5(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}+\\beta {e}^{-x}) & =-9{e}^{5x}-10{e}^{-x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x}+(-1-5)\\beta {e}^{-x} & =-9{e}^{5x}-10{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x}-6\\beta {e}^{-x} & =-9{e}^{5x}-10{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of the two distinct exponentials gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-9,\\qquad -6\\beta =-10.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-9,\\qquad \\beta =\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-9x{e}^{5x}+\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}-9x{e}^{5x}+\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0+\\frac{5}{3} & =4 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{3}{e}^{5x}-9x{e}^{5x}+\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}]}
